= 1
{parent=2010/a/a2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/a/a2/1}

Regard $K$ as a <vector space> over $F$. Since $K$ is finite, a maximal <linearly independent> list has finite length $d$ and is a <basis>. Every element is uniquely a <linear combination> of its $d$ <basis vectors>, with $q$ choices for each coefficient. Hence $\boxed{|K|=q^d}$, with $d=[K:F]$. This includes $d=1$ when the extension is trivial.

= 2
{parent=2010/a/a2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/a/a2/2}

Write $q=p^r$, where $p$ is the <characteristic of a field>[characteristic]. In a <splitting field> of $X^{q^m}-X$ over $F$, let $K$ be its set of roots. The <derivative> of this <polynomial> is $-1$, so all $q^m$ roots are distinct. The <Frobenius endomorphism>[Frobenius] <power function of a statistical test>[power] is additive and multiplicative: $(x+y)^{q^m}=x^{q^m}+y^{q^m}$ and $(xy)^{q^m}=x^{q^m}y^{q^m}$. Thus roots are closed under addition, negatives and multiplication; for a nonzero root, $(x^{-1})^{q^m}=x^{-1}$. They include $0,1$ and form a <field>.

Every $a\in F$ satisfies $a^q=a$, by <Lagrange's theorem for finite groups>[Lagrange's theorem] on $F^\times$ and separately at zero. Iterating gives $a^{q^m}=a$, so $F\subseteq K$. The constructed <field> therefore contains $F$ and has exactly $\boxed{q^m\text{ elements}}$.

= 3
{parent=2010/a/a2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/a/a2/3}

By the allowed cyclicity theorem, $K^\times$ has a generator $z$. Every nonzero element of $K$ is a <power function of a statistical test>[power] of $z$, and zero also belongs to $F(z)$. Thus $K\subseteq F(z)\subseteq K$, giving $\boxed{K=F(z)}$. This is a primitive element argument for <finite fields> using the multiplicative <group>, not a claim that the additive <group> is cyclic.

= 4
{parent=2010/a/a2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/a/a2/4}

For $\alpha\ne0$, <Lagrange's theorem for finite groups>[Lagrange's theorem] in the multiplicative <group> of order $q^m-1$ gives $\alpha^{q^m-1}=1$; at zero the desired <identity element>[identity] is immediate. Thus all elements satisfy $\alpha^{q^m}-\alpha=0$.

For an irreducible <vertex degree>[degree]-$m$ <polynomial> $f\in F[X]$, the quotient $L=F[X]/(f)$ is a <field> of <dimension> $m$, hence has $q^m$ elements by part 1. Its element $\alpha=[X]$ therefore satisfies $\alpha^{q^m}-\alpha=0$. The evaluation homomorphism $F[X]\to L$ has kernel $(f)$, so
$$
\boxed{f(X)\mid X^{q^m}-X.}
$$

= B
{parent=2010}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Group Theory}

= B2
{parent=2010/b}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2010/b/b2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/b/b2/a}

Given $N\trianglelefteq G$ and $H\le G$, the conditions are $\boxed{G=NH,\quad N\cap H=\{1\}}$. Every element then has a unique expression $nh$. The extra datum is the action $\alpha:H\to\operatorname{Aut}(N)$ given by <conjugation>, $\alpha_h(n)=hnh^{-1}$. On pairs $(n,h)$ the reconstructed multiplication is
$$
(n,h)(n',h')=(n\alpha_h(n'),hh').
$$
For example $S_3=\langle(123)\rangle\rtimes\langle(12)\rangle$. The order-three <subgroup> is normal, the intersection is trivial, and their sizes multiply to six. <Conjugation> by $(12)$ inverts $(123)$, so the action is nontrivial. Consequently this is not the direct product of these <subgroups>: $C_3\times C_2$ is <abelian>, whereas $S_3$ is not.

= b
{parent=2010/b/b2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/b/b2/b}

Compose the second map first. Direct substitution gives
$$
\boxed{(A,b)(A',b')=(AA',Ab'+b).}
$$
The result is affine because the product <matrix> is invertible. The <identity element>[identity] is $(I,0)$ and the <inverse element>[inverse] is $(A^{-1},-A^{-1}b)$. <Associativity> follows from composition of maps, or by expanding this product three times. Distinct pairs give distinct maps, since evaluating at zero gives $b$ and evaluating the difference on <basis vectors> gives all columns of $A$. Thus these pairs faithfully describe a <group>.

= c
{parent=2010/b/b2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/b/b2/c}

The map $A\mapsto(A,0)$ is an <injective> <group homomorphism>, so its image is the required copy of $\operatorname{GL}_n(F)$. Translations are the <subgroup> $N=\{(I,b):b\in F^n\}$, isomorphic to the additive <group> of $F^n$. For any affine map,
$$
\boxed{(A,b)(I,t)(A,b)^{-1}=(I,At).}
$$
Thus <conjugation> preserves translations, proving $N\trianglelefteq G$.

= d
{parent=2010/b/b2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/b/b2/d}

Every pair factors as $(A,b)=(I,b)(A,0)$, and a <linear map> which is also a translation fixes zero and has linear part $I$, hence is the <identity element>[identity]. Thus $G=NH$ and $N\cap H=\{1\}$. The <conjugation> formula in (c) says that the action needed for reconstruction is simply the usual <matrix> action $A:t\mapsto At$ on the additive <group> $F^n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{G\cong F^n\rtimes\operatorname{GL}_n(F).}
$$

= e
{parent=2010/b/b2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2010/b/b2/e}

There are $p^n$ choices for the translation. Choose the columns of an <invertible matrix> successively: the first is any nonzero column, the second avoids its span, and at stage $j+1$ one avoids a $j$-dimensional span of size $p^j$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{|G|=p^n\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}(p^n-p^j).}
$$
The successive <linear independence> conditions are exactly the criterion that the columns form a <basis>.

= C
